  "question": {
    "text": "<p>There are two vernier calipers both of which have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> divided into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> equal divisions on the main scale. The Vernier scale of one of the calipers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced separators=\"|\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfenced></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> equal divisions that correspond to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn></math> main scale divisions. The Vernier scale of the other caliper <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced separators=\"|\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> equal divisions that correspond to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn></math> main scale divisions. The readings of the two calipers are shown in the figure. The measured values (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>) by calipers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively, are<br ><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/Phy_Papr2_4_A_.jpg\"/><br ><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/Phy_Papr2_4_B_.jpg\"/></p>",
